---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import P from "../components/P.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { dead } from "../lib/people.js";
import places from "../data/places.json";
import reads from "../data/scilla-reads.json";
import m13 from "../data/scilla-matr-1913.json";
const here = dead.filter((p) => [p.birthPlace, p.deathPlace, p.burialPlace]
  .some((x) => (x || "").startsWith("Scilla")));
const pl = places.find((x) => x.name.startsWith("Scilla")) || {};
const surnames = [...new Set(here.map((p) => p.surname).filter(Boolean))].sort();
---
<Base title="Scilla" eyebrow="Reggio Calabria"
      dek="The rock at the northern gate of the Strait of Messina, and the fishing quarter beneath it. Two of the four grandparents were born here, four years apart, and did not marry until Brisbane.">

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="grid g3">
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{here.length}</div><div class="l">people recorded at Scilla</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{pl.first || "1821"}</div><div class="l">the earliest birth named here</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">2</div><div class="l">of the four quarters begin here</div></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The place</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Scilla is the <em>Scylla</em> of the <em>Odyssey</em> — the rock opposite the whirlpool of
      Charybdis, at the narrowest point of the strait that separates Calabria from Sicily. The
      <strong>Castello Ruffo</strong> sits on the headland; below it, on the water, is
      <strong>Chianalea</strong>, a lane of houses built straight into the sea where the boats are
      moored at the doors. This was a swordfishing town, and it still is.</p>
      <p>The geography is the point for this family. From Chianalea you can see
      <a href={u("/piedimonte-etneo")}>Etna</a> on a clear morning. The Scilla families and the
      Piedimonte Etneo family were looking at each other across thirty kilometres of water for
      generations — and met on the other side of the world.</p>
      <p>Scilla was levelled by the <strong>1783 earthquake</strong> and the tsunami that followed it,
      when a wave took a large part of the population off the beach at Marina Grande. As at
      <a href={u("/briatico")}>Briatico</a>, that is why the record here begins where it does.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The families</h2>
    <p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14px;line-height:2;margin-top:.8rem">
      {surnames.map((s, i) => <>{i > 0 && " · "}<strong>{s}</strong></>)}
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The registers, opened 10 September 2026</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">An index that names the parents — and misleads</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Scilla is <strong>not</strong> in FamilySearch's full-text corpus — four searches
      place-matched to it returned nothing, and its Calabrian holdings are Catanzaro and Vibo
      Valentia. But <strong>Antenati has 57 volumes</strong> from the Archivio di Stato di Reggio
      Calabria, births from <strong>1850</strong>; marriages run <strong>1905–1913 only</strong>.</p>
      <p>The pre-unification <em>Stato civile della restaurazione</em> index is laid out far better
      than the Italian one that replaced it. Its columns are <strong>N. d'ord · Cognomi e Nomi dei
      nati · Patria · Professione · NOMI E COGNOMI DEI GENITORI · giorno della nascita</strong>.
      <strong>The index names the parents</strong> — a whole year's households of one surname read off
      a single opening.</p>
      <p><strong>And it will mislead you if you trust it.</strong> See below.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Act 228 of 1861</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Rocco Arena, born 20 December 1861</h2>
    <div class="hero-plate" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <img src={u("/plates/scilla-act228-arena-rocco.jpg")} alt="Scilla birth register 1861, act 228" />
      <div>
        <blockquote style="margin:0 0 .9rem">L'anno mille ottocento sessant<strong>uno</strong> il dì
        <strong>ventitre</strong> del mese di <strong>Dicembre</strong>… Comune di <strong>Scilla</strong>,
        Distretto di <strong>Reggio</strong>, Provincia della Prima Calabria Ulteriore, è comparso
        <strong>Francesco Antonio Arena</strong>, <strong>figli di Filippo</strong>, di anni
        <strong>quarantadue</strong>, di professione <strong>marinaro</strong>, domiciliato in Scilla…
        è nat[o] da <strong>Serafina Arlotta</strong>, di anni <strong>ventotto</strong>… nel giorno
        <strong>venti</strong> del mese di Dicembre… il nome di <strong>Rocco</strong>.</blockquote>
        <p><strong>Three generations in one act:</strong> <strong>Filippo Arena</strong>, his son
        <strong>Francesco Antonio Arena</strong> — born about 1819, and a <em>marinaro</em>, a sailor —
        and the boy <strong>Rocco</strong>. Witnessed by Giuseppe Idone, 64, and Francesco Antonio
        Polì, 70, <em>proprietario</em>; the parish note records the baptism on the 23rd.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.8rem" class="small">Archivio di Stato di Reggio Calabria, Stato civile
        della restaurazione, Scilla, Nati 1861, atto 228 — Antenati <code>an_ua2002933</code>, image 117.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Act 206 of 1883</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Giovanni Polistena, born 16 September 1883</h2>
    <div class="hero-plate" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <img src={u("/plates/scilla-act206-polistena-giovanni.jpg")} alt="Scilla birth register 1883, act 206" />
      <div>
        <blockquote style="margin:0 0 .9rem">L'anno milleottocento ottant<strong>atre</strong>, addì
        <strong>diciassette</strong> di <strong>Settembre</strong>… Uffiziale dello Stato Civile del
        Comune di <strong>Scilla</strong>, è comparso <strong>Camillo Antonio Polistena</strong>, di
        anni <strong>quarantotto</strong>, <strong>pescatore</strong>, domiciliato in questo Comune…
        nella casa posta in <strong>Via Grotte</strong> al numero <strong>quarantasette</strong>, da
        <strong>Giovanna Romano</strong> sua moglie, <strong>filatrice</strong>, seco lui
        convivente… a cui dà il nome di <strong>Giovanni</strong>.</blockquote>
        <p><P n="Angela Polistena" />'s father, born at half past nine at night in a house on
        <strong>Via Grotte</strong> — the lane of houses standing in the water at
        <strong>Chianalea</strong>. His own father <strong>Camillo Antonio Polistena</strong> was
        forty-eight, so born about <strong>1835</strong>, and a <strong>fisherman</strong>.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.8rem"><strong>The father agrees with the family tree</strong>, which
        calls him Antonio Polistena. <strong>The mother does not.</strong> The register says
        <strong>Giovanna Romano</strong>; the tree says <strong>Giovanna Pontillo</strong> — and gives
        that Giovanna two parents <em>both</em> surnamed Pontillo, which reads like an error in the
        tree rather than a second marriage.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.8rem" class="small">Archivio di Stato di Reggio Calabria, Stato civile
        italiano, Scilla, Nati 1883, atto 206 — Antenati <code>an_ua16601246</code>, image 73.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Act 230 of 1892</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Giuseppe Arena, born 6 October 1892 — and his father is eleven years older than the tree says</h2>
    <div class="hero-plate" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <img src={u("/plates/scilla-act230-arena-giuseppe-1892.jpg")} alt="Scilla birth register 1892, act 230" />
      <div>
        <blockquote style="margin:0 0 .9rem">L'anno mille-ottocento-novant<strong>adue</strong>, addì
        <strong>nove</strong> di <strong>Ottobre</strong>… è comparso <strong>Giovanni Arena</strong>,
        di anni <strong>trentatré</strong>, <strong>pescatore</strong>, domiciliato in
        <strong>Scilla</strong>, il quale mi ha dichiarato che alle ore ante meridiane
        <strong>undici</strong> e minuti <strong>trenta</strong> del dì <strong>sei</strong> del
        corrente mese, nella casa posta in <strong>Via Grotte</strong> al numero
        <strong>settantacinque</strong>, da <strong>Concetta Russo</strong> sua moglie,
        <strong>filatrice</strong>, seco lui convivente… è nato un bambino di sesso maschile… a cui
        dà il nome di <strong>Giuseppe</strong>.</blockquote>
        <p>This is the most useful act the Scilla sweep has produced. It is
        <P n="Rocco Arena" />'s father, and it <strong>confirms the tree on two points and corrects
        it on a third</strong>.</p>
        <div class="rec" style="margin-top:14px">
          <div class="k">Confirmed</div>
          <div class="v"><strong>Giuseppe Arena</strong>, born <strong>6 October 1892</strong> at
          Scilla — the tree had the year, the register gives the day</div><div class="w">agrees</div>
          <div class="k">Confirmed</div>
          <div class="v">his mother <strong>Concetta Russo</strong>, a <em>filatrice</em> — a
          spinner</div><div class="w">agrees</div>
          <div class="k">Corrected</div>
          <div class="v"><strong>Giovanni Arena is thirty-three</strong> in October 1892, so he was
          born about <strong>1859</strong>. The tree says <strong>1870</strong>.</div>
          <div class="w">eleven years</div>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top:1rem">It also gives Giovanni something the tree never did: a trade.
        He is a <strong>pescatore</strong>, a fisherman — and the house is on
        <strong>Via Grotte</strong>, the same lane at Chianalea where
        <a href={u("/people/giovanni-polistena")}>Giovanni Polistena</a> was born nine years earlier
        at number 47. Two of this family's four Calabrian quarters begin in the same street.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.8rem" class="small">Archivio di Stato di Reggio Calabria, Stato civile
        italiano, Scilla, Nati 1892, atto 230 — Antenati <code>an_ua16601257</code>, container
        <code>5Yb6no7</code>, image 79.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Act 81 of 1870</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The Arena boy who was not the one we wanted</h2>
    <div class="hero-plate" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <img src={u("/plates/scilla-act81-arena-1870.jpg")} alt="Scilla birth register 1870, act 81" />
      <div>
        <p>Before act 230 was found, the obvious way to reach Giovanni Arena was to open
        <strong>Scilla, Nati 1870</strong> and look for him. The annual index gave three Arena births
        that year, and exactly one with <em>Giovanni</em> among his names:</p>
        <blockquote style="margin:.9rem 0">…è comparso <strong>Giuseppe Arena di Domenico</strong>, di
        anni <strong>trenta</strong>, <strong>cordaio</strong>, domiciliato e residente in Scilla…
        dalla di lui moglie <strong>Serafina Vita di Giovanni</strong>, di anni <strong>venti</strong>,
        donna di casa… in questo Comune, <strong>Centro Acquagrande</strong>, al quale figlio dichiara
        dare i nomi di <strong>Domenico-Giovanni-Camillo</strong>.</blockquote>
        <p>Three Arena generations in one act — <strong>Domenico</strong>, his son
        <strong>Giuseppe</strong> born about 1840, and the boy. And it was tempting: the tree's
        Giovanni Arena named his own first son <strong>Giuseppe</strong>, which is exactly what
        Calabrian naming custom would predict if his father were a Giuseppe.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.8rem"><strong>It is the wrong man, and three things say so.</strong>
        The marginal annotation records that he married at Scilla on <strong>30 November 1899</strong>
        to <strong>Arena Maria</strong>, born there in 1869 — not to Concetta Russo. The annotation
        calls him <strong>Arena Domenico</strong>, not Giovanni, which the naming custom also
        predicts: Domenico was his father's father, Giovanni only his mother's. And act 230 then
        settled it outright by making the tree's Giovanni eleven years older than this child.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.8rem">His father's trade is worth keeping too. <strong>Giuseppe Arena
        was a <em>cordaio</em>, a ropemaker</strong> — the first man in the Calabrian lines this
        archive has found who does not work the water, though rope is not far from boats.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.8rem" class="small">Archivio di Stato di Reggio Calabria, Stato civile
        italiano, Scilla, Nati 1870, atto 81 — Antenati <code>an_ua16601232</code>, container
        <code>wWqVnPB</code>, image 19. The mother's surname <strong>Vita</strong> is a real Scilla
        surname, not a misreading of <em>Sita</em>: it appears three times in the 1913 marriage index
        below, and <em>Sita</em> never.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">What the registers keep saying</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">They were sea families</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Every man the registers have named in this archive's Calabrian lines works the water:</p>
      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:14px">
        <div class="k">Scilla, 1861</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Francesco Antonio Arena</strong> — <em>marinaro</em></div><div class="w">sailor</div>
        <div class="k">Scilla, 1883</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Camillo Antonio Polistena</strong> — <em>pescatore</em></div><div class="w">fisherman</div>
        <div class="k">Scilla, 1892</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Giovanni Arena</strong> — <em>pescatore</em></div><div class="w">fisherman</div>
        <div class="k">Briatico, 1886</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Alfonso Prostamo</strong> and his father <strong>Domenico</strong> — <em>marinari</em></div><div class="w">sailors</div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">There is now <strong>one exception</strong>, and it is worth
      naming rather than hiding: <strong>Giuseppe Arena</strong>, in 1870, is a <em>cordaio</em> —
      a ropemaker. He is a different Arena household from the tree's, and rope is a harbour trade,
      but he is not on the water. A pattern that admits an exception is a stronger claim than one
      that has never met one.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>The family tree records not one of them as anything.</strong>
      Two provinces, three families, and the same trade — which is exactly what you would expect of
      Chianalea and the Costa degli Dei, and exactly what an undated pedigree cannot tell you.
      The Sicilian <a href={u("/piedimonte-etneo")}>Mazza</a>, up on the mountain, are
      <em>braccianti</em> and <em>contadini</em> to a man. <strong>The archive can now say what these
      people did, which it could not this morning.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Scilla, Nati 1859 — act 46</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Orazio Arena and Giovanna Zirilli exist after all</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>The 1859 volume was opened looking for <strong>Giovanni Arena</strong>, whom act 230 of 1892
      had just relocated from 1870 to about 1859. He is not there. The A section holds exactly three
      Arena births — and the first of them is worth more than the one that was wanted:</p>
      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:16px">
        <div class="k">act 46</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Arena Domenico</strong>, Scilla — <strong>Orazio e Zirilli
        Giovanna</strong> — 10 February 1859</div><div class="w">the couple</div>
        <div class="k">act 111</div>
        <div class="v">Arena Maria, Scilla — Giosafatto e Longordo Rosaria — 1 May 1859</div>
        <div class="w">not in the tree</div>
        <div class="k">act 205</div>
        <div class="v">Arena Annunziato, Scilla — Santo e Gioffrè Giovanna — 15 September 1859</div>
        <div class="w">not in the tree</div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong><P n="Orazio Arena" /> and Giovanna Zirilli are the couple
      the family tree names as <P n="Rocco Arena" />'s parents</strong> — and until now this archive
      had been unable to find either of them in any register at all. The 1861 act said something else
      entirely, and the honest reading was that the tree might simply be wrong. It is not. They were
      at Scilla, and they were having children.</p>
      <p><strong>What this does and does not settle.</strong> It does not make Rocco their son: the
      1861 register contains exactly one Rocco Arena and he belongs to Francesco Antonio Arena,
      <em>marinaro</em>, and Serafina Arlotta. What it does is move the likely error from the
      <em>parents</em> to the <em>year</em> — and this archive has now caught the tree six years out
      on Salvatore Mazza, six on Orazio Mazza and <strong>eleven on Giovanni Arena</strong>. A Rocco
      Arena di Orazio in some other year is now a specific, findable thing rather than a hope.</p>
      <p class="small" style="margin-top:.8rem">Archivio di Stato di Reggio Calabria, Stato civile
      della restaurazione, Scilla, Nati 1859 — Antenati <code>an_ua2002928</code>, container
      <code>5vEYzey</code>, index at images 156–163. Read from the <strong>whole uncropped
      page</strong>: after the 1861 misalignment, this archive no longer reads parentage off a
      cropped index column, because on a full page you can see for yourself that the child's name and
      its parents sit on the same ruled line.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Scilla, Nati 1851 — the Bourbon index, which names the parents</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Michele Donato is not in the year the tree gives him</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>The pre-unification <em>Tavola annuale alfabetica de' Nati</em> is the best-designed index in
      this archive's whole territory: <strong>Num. d'ordine · Cognomi e Nomi de' Nati · Patria ·
      Cognomi e Nomi de' Genitori · Giorno della Nascita</strong>. It <strong>names both
      parents</strong>, so a whole year's households can be read off one opening without opening a
      single act. For <P n="Michele Donato" /> — <P n="Nunnziata Donato" />'s father, who sailed from
      Palermo and landed at New Orleans on 11 March 1908 — it should have been one crop.</p>
      <p><strong>The D section of 1851 runs from entry 65 to entry 75, and there is no Donato in
      it.</strong> Eleven births: seven Di Franco, two Dormì, one De Marco, one De Alessandro. Entry
      64 is the last C and entry 76 begins the F, so the block is complete and nothing is hidden by a
      page turn.</p>
      <p>Two explanations are open, and this archive has evidence for only one of them.
      <strong>The tree's birth years have been wrong before</strong> — six years out on Salvatore
      Mazza, six on Orazio Mazza, and, as of act 230 above, <strong>eleven years out on Giovanni
      Arena</strong>. His eldest recorded son was born in 1878, which puts his own birth anywhere from
      about 1848 to 1858. A wrong year is likelier than a wrong town, but that is a hypothesis and it
      is filed as one.</p>
      <p class="small" style="margin-top:.8rem">The same opening yields one further fact worth
      keeping: <strong>the 1851 A section holds a single Arena birth</strong> — Arena Maria, of
      Pasquale, at Scilla. The Arena of Scilla were not numerous in the 1850s.</p>
      <p class="small" style="margin-top:.8rem">Archivio di Stato di Reggio Calabria, Stato civile
      della restaurazione, Scilla, Nati 1851 — Antenati <code>an_ua2002901</code>, container
      <code>LeJQDD6</code>, index at images 123–131. <strong>These scans are small</strong> — about
      1000 × 1500 pixels, against 4600 × 3700 for the Italian-era volumes — which is why the IIIF
      request must ask for <code>/full/</code> and not a width, and why the 1861 index misled this
      archive earlier in the day.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Scilla, Matrimoni 1913 — the whole index</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Fifty-eight marriages, and four surnames that are not among them</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Antenati's Scilla marriage series runs <strong>1905–1913 only</strong>. The 1913 volume's
      container was already in hand, and — unlike the portal's HTML, which rate-limits after about
      three page loads — <strong>the IIIF images are not throttled at all</strong>. So all eleven
      index openings came down for nothing, and the year is transcribed here in full.</p>
      <p>The reason for opening it was <a href={u("/people/giovanni-polistena")}>Giovanni
      Polistena</a>'s marriage to <strong>Nunziata Donato</strong>, which would name four of
      <P n="Angela Polistena" />'s grandparents in a single act. <strong>It is not here.</strong>
      Neither is any Arena, any Donato, or any Mazza:</p>
      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:16px">
        {Object.entries(m13.wanted).map(([k, v]) => (
          <>
            <div class="k">{k}</div>
            <div class="v">{v === 0
              ? <>absent — <strong>no {k} married at Scilla in 1913</strong></>
              : <>{v} {v === 1 ? "marriage" : "marriages"}</>}</div>
            <div class="w">{v === 0 ? "closes a search" : "of 58"}</div>
          </>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>A negative result is still a result</strong>, and this one
      is cheap to state and expensive to re-derive. The Polistena–Donato marriage is either outside
      1905–13, or it was not celebrated at Scilla. Eight volumes remain in the window; this archive
      has done one.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:.8rem">The surname distribution is its own small finding. Fifty-eight
      couples produced <strong>{m13.surnames.length} surnames</strong>, and the commonest of them —
      {m13.surnames.slice(0, 6).map(([n, c], i) => <>{i > 0 && ", "}<strong>{n}</strong> ({c})</>)} —
      is the population the Arena, Polistena and Donato households were marrying into.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:20px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Groom</th><th>of</th><th>Bride</th><th>of</th><th class="m">Date</th><th class="m">Act</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {m13.rows.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{r.groom}</td><td class="small">{r.groomFather}</td>
              <td>{r.bride}</td><td class="small">{r.brideFather}</td>
              <td class="m">{r.date}</td>
              <td class="m">{r.act}{r.parte !== "—" && r.parte !== "I" ? " · " + r.parte : ""}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:22px">One entry was worth opening the act for. Index no. 32 read
    as a possible <strong>Mazza</strong> bride — which would have put the Sicilian surname in Scilla,
    a genuinely surprising thing. <strong>Act 31 says <em>Muzzi</em>.</strong> Majori Carmelo, 27, a
    carpenter born at Gallico, married <strong>Muzzi Santa Maria</strong>, 24, of Scilla, on
    20 September 1913. Read the act, not the index.</p>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:14px">Archivio di Stato di Reggio Calabria, Stato civile
    italiano, Scilla, Matrimoni 1913 — Antenati <code>an_ua16594965</code>, container
    <code>5K7arD3</code>, index at images 75–85.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">A correction, in the open</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The index said something else, and the caution caught it</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Reading the 1861 index, this archive recorded Rocco Arena's parents as
      <strong>«Giuseppe e Zirilli Grazia»</strong> — and noted that while the rare surname
      <em>Zirilli</em> agreed with the family tree's <em>Giovana Zirilli</em>, both forenames did not.
      It was filed as a <strong>candidate, not a join</strong>, with the words <em>"read the act
      itself before joining"</em>.</p>
      <p><strong>The act says Francesco Antonio Arena and Serafina Arlotta.</strong> The index's
      parents column had been misaligned by one row: it is a wide ruled table photographed across a
      gutter, and once the columns are cropped apart the row a name sits on is not reliably the row
      its parents sit on.</p>
      <p><strong>That caution is the only reason the archive does not now carry a wrong set of
      great-great-grandparents.</strong> Had the surname agreement been treated as sufficient — as
      the <a href={u("/register-1879")}>Venerio reading</a> was, earlier the same day — it would have
      been merged and published.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">And an unresolved conflict</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">This is not the tree's Rocco Arena</h2>
    <div class="rec" style="margin-top:16px">
      <div class="k">The tree says</div>
      <div class="v">Rocco Arena, b. 1861, son of <strong>Orazio Arena</strong> and
      <strong>Giovana Zirilli</strong></div><div class="w">no source</div>
      <div class="k">The register says</div>
      <div class="v">Rocco Arena, b. <strong>20 December 1861</strong>, son of
      <strong>Francesco Antonio Arena</strong>, marinaro, and <strong>Serafina Arlotta</strong></div>
      <div class="w">atto 228</div>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:22px">The 1861 Scilla register contains <strong>exactly one
    Rocco Arena</strong>, and <strong>neither parent matches</strong>. So either the tree's parents
    are wrong, or its birth year is. This archive has already found the same tree
    <strong>six years out</strong> on Salvatore Mazza's birth and six on Orazio Mazza's, so the year
    is the likelier error — but that is a hypothesis, not a finding, and the Arena line stays where it
    was until a record settles it. <a href={u("/open-questions")}>The queue →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>Everyone the archive places here</h2>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th class="m">Dates</th><th>Born</th><th>Died</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {here.sort((a, b) => (a.born || 9999) - (b.born || 9999)).map((p) => (
            <tr>
              <td><a href={u("/people/" + p.slug)}>{p.name}</a></td>
              <td class="m">{[p.born, p.died].filter(Boolean).join("–")}</td>
              <td>{p.birthPlace}</td>
              <td>{p.deathPlace}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
